<details class="guide" id="guide">
<summary><h2>Guide</h2><span class="muted">What the grades mean</span></summary>
<p>Every block gets one grade. The worst problem found sets it, so a calm, well-aligned wind still grades Dangerous if a thunderstorm is likely. With no problems, the thermals decide between Poor, Ok, Good and Strong. The limits below are for <span class="g g-pg">paragliders</span><span class="g g-hg">hang gliders</span>; change glider in the settings menu.</p>
{% for g in gliders %}<ul class="guide g g-{{ g.id }}">
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}ok" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill ok"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.ok }}</span> Ok</span> Flyable, with decent thermals. The wind at launch is in the green direction (or no more than {{ legend.margin }} degrees outside it, which is marginally crossed: you can usually still get off, so it is noted but does not lower the grade) and below {{ limits[g.id].orange }}, gusts are below {{ legend.gust_orange }}, there is no rain or storm risk, and thermals are decent (quality at least {{ legend.quality }}%, reaching at least {{ legend.min_height }}).</li>
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}good" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill good"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.good }}</span> Good</span> A time you definitely want to go flying. Strong, workable thermals (quality at least {{ legend.good_quality }}% and an updraft of {{ legend.a_good_updraft if uses_ausrasp else legend.good_updraft }} or more) and the wind comfortably below {{ limits[g.id].strong }}.</li>
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}strong" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill strong"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.strong }}</span> Strong</span> Powerful, and it demands experience. Very strong thermals (an updraft of {{ legend.a_strong_updraft if uses_ausrasp else legend.strong_updraft }} or more), or strong thermals with a brisk wind of {{ limits[g.id].strong }} or more. Rewarding for experienced pilots; not a time for novices.</li>
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}poor" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill poor"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.poor }}</span> Poor</span> Nothing dangerous, but not worth it, except maybe  for sleddies. The wind is too light (under {{ legend.green_from }}) or outside the green direction, thermals are weak or low (quality under {{ legend.quality }}%), or rain is expected ({{ legend.rain_light }} or more).</li>
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}turbulent" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill turbulent"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.turbulent }}</span> Turbulent</span> Rough air likely. Wind of {{ limits[g.id].orange }} or more, gusts of {{ legend.gust_orange }} or more, or storm energy of {{ legend.cape_overdevelop }} J/kg or more.</li>
<li id="guide-{{ '' if g.id == 'pg' else 'hg-' }}dangerous" tabindex="-1"><span class="pill dangerous"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.dangerous }}</span> Dangerous</span> Do not fly. Wind of {{ limits[g.id].red }} or more, gusts of {{ legend.gust_red }} or more, strong wind well outside the green direction, heavy rain ({{ legend.rain_heavy }} or more), or a thunderstorm risk (storm energy of {{ legend.cape_storm }} J/kg or more, or {{ legend.cape_overdevelop }} J/kg or more with rain).</li>
</ul>{% endfor %}
<h3>What the grade takes into account</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind.</strong> Speed and direction at launch altitude ({{ launch_alt }}), and for speed also the stronger of the lower winds (10 m and 850 hPa). The green direction is from {{ legend.sector_from }} to {{ legend.sector_to }}. Speeds are averages. Hang gliders cope with more wind than paragliders, so the wind limits differ; everything else is the same.</li>
<li><strong>Wind arrow.</strong> The triangle in each day shows where the launch wind is blowing: green inside the flyable direction, amber just outside it (marginally crossed: noted, but it does not lower the grade), red outside it.</li>
<li><strong>Gusts.</strong> The model's forecast gust speed near the ground (about 10 m up) at its grid point, not at launch altitude, so the page shows it directly under the ground wind it goes with.</li>
<li><strong>Rain and storms.</strong> Rain rate, and storm energy (CAPE), which shows how likely thunderstorms and strong gust fronts are.</li>
<li><strong>Thermals.</strong> Strength and height, which separate Good from Poor.{% if uses_ausrasp %} Height and updraft are <a href="{{ links.ausrasp_vic }}">AUSRASP</a>'s forecast for {{ ausrasp_area }}. AUSRASP publishes the updraft in whole metres per second, so Good needs {{ legend.a_good_updraft }} or more and Strong {{ legend.a_strong_updraft }} or more.{% if some_estimate %} Where AUSRASP's figures are not available a block uses the page's own estimate from the global model, named in the line under the title; the estimate uses {{ legend.good_updraft }} and {{ legend.strong_updraft }}.{% endif %}{% else %} The height is where usable thermals reach, about half the depth of the boundary layer above launch (not the top of it), and is launch height when the updraft is below {{ legend.critical }}, because thermals that weak are not usable.{% endif %}</li>
<li><strong>Turbulence is not measured.</strong> It is inferred from wind strength, gusts and storm energy. Wind shear (the difference between ground and upper wind) is shown in the details for information but does not change the grade.</li>
<li><strong>Limitations.</strong> The grades come from computer models on a grid, not from measurements. It cannot see local ridge or valley effects, so check the live station chart and the Bureau of Meteorology warnings before you go. Days 5 to 7 use the same rules on rougher values.</li>
</ul>
</details>

<details class="guide" id="method">
<summary><h2>How the numbers are calculated</h2><span class="muted">Where the data comes from and how it is turned into thermal figures</span></summary>

<h3>Where the data comes from</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind, gusts, rain, storms and temperature.</strong> {{ model }}, run {{ cycle_label }}. This is NOAA's GFS global weather model, published free by NOAA, on a grid of about 25 km. The page reads the grid point nearest the launch: wind at 10 m and at four pressure levels above it (interpolated to launch altitude), gusts, rain rate, storm energy (CAPE) and the temperature at 2 m. {% if not uses_ausrasp %}It also reads the height of the boundary layer (the layer of air stirred up by the ground's heat) and the heat flowing into the air from the ground, which give the thermal estimate below. {% endif %}Nothing in the forecast is measured: it is all model output.</li>
{% if uses_ausrasp %}<li><strong>Thermal height and updraft.</strong> From <a href="{{ links.ausrasp }}">AUSRASP</a>'s <a href="{{ links.ausrasp_vic }}">Victoria forecast</a>, which runs a 4 km regional weather model (WRF) started from the same global model and works out thermal figures from it. The page reads two of AUSRASP's published values for {{ ausrasp_area }}: <em>thermalling height</em> (the lowest of the height where the average updraft falls to 225 feet a minute, cumulus cloudbase and overdevelopment cloudbase, in metres above sea level) and the thermal <em>updraft velocity</em> (W*), which AUSRASP publishes in whole metres per second. Each block uses the AUSRASP hour valid at its start time. The run{% if ausrasp_runs|length > 1 %}s{% endif %} behind this page started {{ ausrasp_runs|join(", ") }}; AUSRASP re-runs each day separately, and the page checks for new runs through the day. AUSRASP is not used for wind or rain. {% if some_estimate %}The days the line under the title says use the estimate are the ones AUSRASP could not supply (not available, incomplete or too old); they use the calculation below. {% endif %}The page shows AUSRASP's figures, not its maps; see them at <a href="{{ links.ausrasp_vic }}">ausrasp.com</a>.</li>{% endif %}
<li><strong>Today at Mystic.</strong> Current conditions and today's hourly forecast come from <a href="{{ links.openmeteo }}">Open-Meteo</a>, loaded by your browser. They are a model estimate for the launch, not a measurement.</li>
<li><strong>The weather station chart.</strong> This is the one measured source: wind recorded at the launch by the club's station and shown by <a href="{{ links.freeflightwx }}">FreeFlightWx</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The grading limits.</strong> The wind bands and flyable direction come from the North East Victorian Hang Gliding Club's published Mystic site settings (set for an intermediate pilot on a low-end B paraglider).</li>
<li><strong>Acknowledgement.</strong> AUSRASP's <a href="{{ links.ausrasp_vic }}">Victoria forecast</a> is supported by the <a href="{{ links.vhpa }}">Victorian Hang Gliding and Paragliding Association (VHPA)</a>, and AUSRASP is run on donations.{% if not uses_ausrasp %} The estimate's constants below were checked against AUSRASP forecasts.{% endif %}</li>
</ul>

<h3>How each thermal figure is worked out</h3>
{% if uses_ausrasp %}<p>Steps 2 to 4 are the page's own estimate from the global model. They are used only for blocks AUSRASP does not cover; blocks with AUSRASP figures take height and updraft from AUSRASP and use the other steps as written.</p>{% endif %}
<ol>
<li><strong>Wind at launch ({{ launch_alt }}).</strong> The model gives the wind at the surface and at fixed pressure levels. The page interpolates between them to the launch altitude, ignoring any level below the model's own ground.</li>
<li><strong>Heating at that hour.</strong> GFS reports the heat going into the air from the ground as an average since the last multiple of 6 hours. The page works out the value for each hour from two consecutive averages, so the late afternoon is not overstated and the morning not understated.</li>
<li><strong>Updraft strength.</strong> The convective velocity scale: the cube root of (gravity divided by air temperature) times the heating (per unit of air density and heat capacity) times the boundary layer depth. Stronger heating and a deeper layer both make stronger thermals. It is the average strength of a dry thermal; clouds can make real thermals stronger. It is zero when the ground is not heating the air.</li>
<li><strong>Thermal height.</strong> The height above sea level that usable thermals reach: launch height ({{ launch_alt }}) plus {{ legend.depth_pct }}% of the boundary layer depth, or launch height itself when the updraft is below {{ legend.critical }}, the critical value for a usable thermal. It is lower than the top of the boundary layer, which is how high smoke would rise rather than how high a glider can climb. Heights are shown rounded down to the nearest 100 m (or 100 ft in feet) because the forecast is not that precise.</li>
<li><strong>Thermal quality.</strong> 100% at an updraft of {{ legend.quality_full }}, in proportion below that, minus {{ legend.penalty }} of wind above {{ legend.wind_threshold }} (the stronger of the 10 m and 850 hPa winds), because wind breaks thermals up. Rounded to the nearest 10%.{% if uses_ausrasp %} With AUSRASP's whole-number updraft, quality takes only a few values.{% endif %}</li>
<li><strong>Temperature at thermal height.</strong> The ground temperature less 9.8 &deg;C for every kilometre climbed (the dry rate of cooling) from launch to the thermal height shown.</li>
<li><strong>Rain, gusts and storm energy.</strong> Taken directly from the model's value for that hour.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>{% if uses_ausrasp %}The wind, gust, rain and temperature figures come from a global model with a grid of about 25 km, which{% else %}A 25 km grid{% endif %} cannot see individual ridges, valleys or the real ground height at the launch, so winds there are usually lighter {% if not uses_ausrasp %}and thermals less detailed {% endif %}than the real thing. Check the station chart on the day.</li>
{% if not uses_ausrasp or some_estimate %}<li>The estimate's constants were fitted to a small comparison, so they are reasonable, not proven.</li>{% endif %}
{% if uses_ausrasp %}<li>AUSRASP is an independent, volunteer-run service. Its figures are for 4 km grid cells over steep ground, so they can differ from what you find at the launch.{% if cell_radius %} The page takes the highest of the cells around the launch to get a better indication of the best thermal conditions over the mountain; the height and the updraft are each the highest in that block, which may be different cells.{% endif %} The page cannot tell you how old a run is beyond the time shown, and uses a run for at most 36 hours after it started.</li>{% endif %}
<li>These are forecasts of average conditions. Individual thermals vary widely around them.</li>
</ul>
</details>

<footer>
<p>Official warnings: <a href="{{ links.bom_warnings }}">Bureau of Meteorology, Victoria</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Advisory only.</strong> This is a computer forecast, not a safety guarantee. Check conditions on the day and use your own judgement.</p>
<p class="muted">The wind speed and wind direction rules are based on the published Mystic site settings of the
<a href="{{ links.nevhgc }}">North East Victorian Hang Gliding Club (NEVHGC)</a>, as shown on their
